9.48 GB`.** ⚠️ **Read the DIRECTION before the number: the REF COUNT ROSE while the BYTES FELL, so this is NOT growth and must not be reported as such; and per F-2563-1 do NOT sum the per-ref figures, which double-count. The new members include today's live `heat14/era6-reride` (F-2625-1).** 🔺 **F-RPG-18** A14 the Regatta's racing body (a spec slice first) · 🔺 **F-RPG-10** A15 the Long Road's convoy stop (default stands until he plays it) · 🔺 **F-RPG-11** A16 Moth Season's choice (default stands until he plays it). 🔺 **F-HEAT14-7** — **the county’s hour never rolls, so a rider can be locked out of the board for good (carried s2629; born in the attended heat-14 drain, on no desk until now — F-2629-1).** `functions/api/standings.ts:166` caps an anon at `30` submissions per `RATE_TTL_SECONDS = 3600`, and `functions/api/_ratelimit.ts` `bumpCounter` re-arms that hour on EVERY accepted write — verified by reading both files, not inherited — so under sustained traffic the window never expires: ride 37’s own reel (`e10-archive-world`, a first-ever secure) was refused `rate_limited`. The row offers three options and I recommend **(b) stop re-arming the TTL, so the hour is a real hour**: (a) raising the cap above the board count re-breaks at the next larger heat and makes a rate limit track the board size, (c) an exempt operator harness adds a privileged path to the public door and un-counts exactly the traffic you would want counted, and (b) is the only one that leaves the stated law — 30 per hour — TRUE with no new surface and no re-tuning. It is a small fix (`bumpCounter` stores the window start beside the count and puts with the REMAINING ttl), and it is fire-authorable the moment you rule; it is player-facing, which is why it is a word and not a drive-by.
- **s2630 handoff (line-1 archive):** Last updated: 2026-09-19T02:33Z s2630 handoff, lock CLEARED — 🧹 **DRY BOTH WAYS FOR THE NINETY-FIFTH CONSECUTIVE FIRE, AND I SPENT IT DISCHARGING THE ONE PRICED JOB MY PREDECESSOR LEFT.** `dry-board-probe` 60 subjects / **0 real drains / 0 UNKNOWN** / 12 closed; all four lanes `ahead=0` (`lane-d` carries the known `tracked-dirt=26`); queued 0 / in-flight 0; runner **ALIVE**. CODEX-WALL read **AT SOURCE** — still **POLICY**, not quota, and its narrow art exception is **SPENT** by its own text. No refill, no dispatch, nothing drained. 🔬 **F-2630-1 — I RAN THE PRICING s2628 ASKED FOR, AND THE ANSWER IS A MEASURED NO (`88f5e7d46`).** s2628 left the `desk-birth-guard` widening priced and unclaimed with an explicit condition: *"run the s1533 pricing over the same corpus FIRST; do not widen on the strength of my one instance."* Replayed over **1,921 session windows** by DRIVING THE GUARD’S OWN EXPORTS rather than reimplementing them (s2348’s correction of s2347, whose two errors both came from a private reimplementation), windows selected by F-2260-1’s identity rule. 📊 **RESULT: the naive widening admits `86` rows and produces `24` NEW FAIL verdicts, of which `16` name ANOTHER thread entirely — a `66.7%` key-drift false-positive floor, ~2.9× the 23% s1533 figure s2628 declined on.** And `66.7%` is a FLOOR because my SELF/OTHER proxy over-credits: `s1333:F-1333-3` scores SELF only because its own header is the nearest preceding id, while the sentence’s subject is one of s1332’s findings. Read strictly, drift is ≥ 17/24. One admission keys on the slug **`building`**, which is not an id at all. 🎯 **The self-referential remainder does not rescue it either:** `F-1055-2` and `F-2432-3` are rows RECORDING that a thread is ALREADY desked — F-2259-1’s exact 3-new-verdicts-ALL-FALSE shape, where this guard’s remedy MANUFACTURES desk items out of handled threads. A self-reference-tightened arm still prices at ~`37–62%` against a `0 in 15` bar. ✅ 