/Users/robin/Claude/Projects/Gold Rush/tasks/BACKLOG.md:3537:✅ **F-1286-2 (s1286, CLOSED-BY-REPAIR s1339 — the gate F-1338-1 wrote was run, and it refuted both of the options that gate itself offered; the cure came from a third mechanism neither finding had considered. Evidence: `reviews/f-1286-2-bark-determinism.md`.)** 📊 **THE GATE'S LITERAL QUESTION, ANSWERED: the occlusion is TRANSIENT — it self-recovers in 8/8 probe runs, never steady-state — and the latency is what makes it a coin flip.** Time from the assertion instant to the tavernkeeper owning the card: **0.97 · 1.63 · 1.63 · 2.18 · 2.23 · 2.32 · 3.28 · 6.27 s** (n=8, both projects, `--workers=1`, idle machine). **Exactly one of eight clears the 5000 ms default deadline — 12.5%, statistically indistinguishable from the 1/6 this row filed.** So the flake is a deadline set below a latency tail, and the tail is what nobody had ever sampled. 🔍 **AND THE OCCLUDER IS NOT WHO EITHER FINDING SAID IT WAS.** This row diagnosed a *"rotating-bark-carousel race"*; F-1338-1 reasoned about *"a youngster passing through"*. **At the assertion instant the card belonged to the NEWSIE in 8/8 runs — never a youngster.** Chen Mei is *anchored to the tavern* and loops its trail (`src/town/townsfolk.ts:198-211`, `seconds: 19, phase: 0.16, pauses: {0: 4}`) — a co-located resident, not a passer-by. The youngsters do occlude, but later, in the 3–11 s band. ⛔ **BOTH OPTIONS THE GATE OFFERED ARE REFUTED BY THE SAME PROBE.** ⑴ *Wait on the world condition* (`__GR_TOWN_DIAGNOSTICS__.activeBark.actorId`): **0 divergences between the diagnostic and the DOM attribute across ~1,900 samples** — same information, same clock, so it is a longer wait wearing a different name. And no deadline is derivable anyway: the probe measured a **16,019 ms** newsie-owned episode at the same spot, which nothing inside the test's 30 s budget clears. ⑵ *Make the approach deterministic* — refuted **in its obvious form**: teleporting to the tavernkeeper's `TOWN_ACTORS` coordinate held for **1 sample of 235** before the hero drifted up to 3.67 world units away. ✅ **THE CURE IS THE THIRD THING, and it is what the failed attempt taught: the coordinate was wrong, not the strategy.** `townActorPlazaPlacement` (`src/town/TownScene.ts:3618`) **overrides the definition position for loop-less actors**, so the tavernkeeper's real post is `(-6.5, -4.85)`, not the literal `(-5.4, -6.75)` — and the literal sits *inside the tavern footprint*, where collision ejects the hero. At the true post the hero holds, `distanceSq = 0`, and the selection at `TownScene.ts:1444` is a **strict `<`**, so no actor can displace it: **700/702 samples owned, first ownership ≤ 220 ms, prompt held `tavern` throughout.** 🔧 **Repair is TEST-ONLY, zero `src/` bytes, and hardcodes nothing** — `standAtTownActor()` READS the live post from `__GR_TOWN_DIAGNOSTICS__.actors[]` (`TownScene.ts:2257-2269`, already exposed), so the placement rule cannot rot out from under it. 📊 **Cure measured at the defect's own denominator** — the three encyclopedia specs batched, `--workers=1`, `--repeat-each=3`, both projects, the identical 42-instance battery that produced this row's 1/6 and F-1337-1's 3/6: **42/42 GREEN, twice (84/84 instances, 12 of them this test).** `tsc --noEmit` clean. 💡 **THE CLASS, and it is s1338's lesson turned on its head: s1338 found a gate whose IMPERATIVE was wrong while its claim was right. Here the imperative was wrong AND the two alternatives the gate offered as replacements were BOTH wrong — because every one of them was reasoned from an unmeasured premise about WHO owned the card.** Three fires described this flake, each more carefully than the last, and all three named the wrong actor. **A gate that enumerates the options also fences in the answer; running it is what lets a fourth option exist.** 🪞 **And the probe caught its own author the same way** — it teleported to the coordinate printed in the definition file, twice, and produced a confident refutation of the strategy that turned out to be a refutation of the coordinate. *A measurement aimed at the wrong subject does not read as noise; it reads as a result.* **ORIGINAL, retained verbatim: OPEN — RE-MEASURED s1337: CONFIRMED AT SOURCE, RATE NOW ON THE RECORD, AND ITS SCOPE WAS ONE TEST TOO NARROW).** s1337 ran the gate this row asked for, on the same idle window that discharged F-1287-1: the three encyclopedia specs batched, `--workers=1`, `--repeat-each=3`, both projects = **42 instances, 5.6 min**. ✓ **The finding reproduces exactly as filed, mechanism and all:** *"EN-02 town board and bark discover contracts and townsfolk"* failed **1/6** (~17%, mobile in this sample) with `toHaveAttribute('data-actor-id','tavernkeeper')` **Received: `youngster_b`** — the rotating-bark-carousel race this row diagnosed, not a discovery failure. **Its repair is still owed and its warning still stands: do not widen the wait — await the named actor.** ⚠️ **But the batch holds a SECOND flake this row never named, and it is three times worse — see F-1337-1.** This row says the batch *"failed once per batch run"*, singular, which reads as *the* unstable test; there are two, and the louder one is undocumented. 💡 **Same class as F-1287-1 one level up:** that row's `MOBILE-ONLY` was a scope claim about *projects*; this row's *"failed once"* is a scope claim about *which test* — both are summaries of the runs somebody happened to do, and neither was ever re-run until the gate forced it. **ORIGINAL FINDING, s1286, retained verbatim:**
