ut, the fix that hides it.”* 🔬 **Mechanism proven from control flow, then measured.** *“Timeout 5000ms exceeded while waiting on the predicate”* is `expect.poll`’s own wording, and `:406`/`:421` were the **only two polls in the test left at the 5000ms default** while every neighbour waiting on the same async game loop had been given 8_000 or 12_000. `storyHintCount` reads persisted `hintsSeen`, written **only inside `StoryRuntime.show()`** (`src/story/StoryRuntime.ts:134`) — so the write is gated behind the story-card **queue**, and that queue’s constants are `CARD_MS = 6000` + `GAP_MS = 3000` (`:9-10`). **The wait has a structural floor up to 9s; the deadline was set below it.** 📊 **Instrumented at a 30s horizon under the identical batch: elapsed = 5047 · 5064 · 5106 · 5024 ms, 42/42 passed.** Every sample clears 5000 by **24–106 ms** — a ~1% straddle, which is precisely why it read as a coin flip and not a hard red. ⚠️ **So the prohibition was aimed at a mechanism that was not there:** a timeout raise cannot fix *starvation*, true — but this is **a deadline provisioned under a deterministic scheduler floor**, and for that the timeout is the honest knob. ✅ **Repaired to `12_000`, derived from the mechanism (clears the 9s max) and matching its neighbours — not tuned until green. Cure measured at the defect’s own denominator: `:381` 6/6 GREEN (was 3/6).** `tsc --noEmit` clean. 🆕 **(D) F-1338-1 FILED — F-1286-2’s OWED REPAIR HAS BEEN WRONG SINCE s1286, AND BEING RE-CONFIRMED TWICE IS WHAT KEPT IT ALIVE.** Its closing instruction is *“do not widen the wait — await the named actor.”* ✓ The finding is real; `:323` failed **1/6** in my batch, a third independent reproduction. ✗ **The cure is a restatement.** `town-bark-card` is a **single persistent element** (`src/town/TownScene.ts:370`) whose `dataset.actorId` is overwritten at `:1519` with `nearest.definition.id` — **there is no carousel of per-actor cards to select between**, so a locator naming the actor waits on the