n"* PASSED and the `>= 9` line was the **ONLY** red. **Cured: the floor is now a CEILING** (`<= 9`, may shrink to 0 freely, may never grow without a measurement), plus a new MANUFACTURED test proving the emptied guard still reaches exit 1. **The grandfather list is now 0 and the guard is STRICT: 43 desk ids, 43 rows, 0 undeclared.** **F-1335-5:** that same test file leaked **14 temp dirs per run** and had been reddening `fixture-teardown` — and therefore **all of `test:node-guards`** — since the moment it landed. Attributed with a CLEAN control after a contaminated one (restoring only the test file reds for a *different* reason; both files at HEAD gives the true 14). **It was invisible to s1334 because the prescribed last act is `test:ledger-guards`, correctly narrowed, which does not include the teardown guard — shipping a new TEST FILE is a late mutation of gate topology that the narrowed battery cannot see.** Cured → **full `test:node-guards` rc=0**; the first fix reddened it a *second* way (hoisting the prefix defeated the guard's literal-extracting regex) so the literals are pinned at each call site with a comment. ⚠️ **(F) AND MY OWN ROWS BROKE A THIRD GUARD, WHICH IS THE MOST REUSABLE THING HERE (F-1335-4).** `desk-declaration-guard` admits a row via **the FIRST** F-ID in the 90-char zone; `findings-state-guard.scan()` collects **EVERY** F-ID in that same zone and assigns them all the row's state. **So citing finding X in your ✅ row's headline does not give X a row — but it does mark X CLOSED.** My `✅ F-1335-1 (… F-1334-2'S GUARD …)` silently closed **F-1334-2** and poisoned **9** blocker-panel rows (`closed-on-panel 9` → rc=1); removing the citation from the zone restored **0**. **Interim discipline applied: a ✅ row's first 90 chars name its own id and no other.** ✅ **(G) DUTIES, EACH CHECKED AT ITS OWN SOURCE.** **ASSAYER 0** (`pending/` empty). **TICKER 0 owed** (`ticker-digest-2026-07-31.md` covers yesterday). **GAZETTE 0, correctly** — nothing player-visible merged; the newsie fix is a test literal. **DEPLOY skipped LAWFULLY** — no gameplay code. **ART-SLOT audit not owed** (never touched the slot) **but I ran it anyway to hold my own F-1120-2 row to the standard I was preaching, and it confirmed rather than inherited: AT RISK 748 files / 566.47 MB, LOCAL-ONLY 0.** `tsc --noEmit` clean. **BACKUP pushed.** **`test:ledger-guards` as my last act per F-1300-4.** 🏜️ **(H) PIPELINE-DRY: lane-a, lane-c, lane-d unchanged; lane-b still HOLDS its five owner-gated files. I authored no master** — every candidate remains shipped, owner-gated, or blocked. ➡️ **(I) NEXT FIRE, IN ORDER. (1) F-1330-1 is still the cheapest word on the board (ONE WORD unblocks green branch `7c4f132f`).** **(2) The desk is now 43/43 visible and the guard is strict — so the highest-value act is no longer backfilling rows, it is AUDITING THE REMAINING DESK ITEMS FOR CURRENCY the way §B did.** Two of nine were not owner-gated at all, and one ask was unrunnable; that rate says the desk holds more. **Start with the FACT-shaped gates** (*"is X still outdated?"*, *"is Y in lore/?"*) — those are unmeasured, not owner-gated. **(3) Do NOT rebuild the marker-equality guard (F-1334-1 refuted it) and do NOT re-derive the 9-row backfill — it is done.** **(4) `logs/suite-red-inventory.md` rows 148/149/408 are now STALE** (gz-h1-newsie:114 is green); it is generated from a full-suite run, so it self-corrects on the next regeneration — do not hand-edit it. **(5) Do NOT reset lane-b.** **(6) §I is a hypothesis like every §I before it.** 🔺 **OWNER DESK — the board is DRY on all four lanes and unblocking it remains entirely owner-side.** **CLOSED this fire: F-1185-1** (premise refuted, red cured — no ruling was ever needed). **New: 🟡 F-1335-2 (`npm install -g @openai/codex@latest` — the corrected 152nd ask) · 🟡 F-1335-4** (rule the arity asymmetry). **F-1335-1 / F-1335-5 / F-1335-6 shipped same fire, no ask.** Carried: 🔺 **F-1334-2** · 🔺 **F-1333-2 superseded** · 🔺 **F-1332-2** · 🟡 **F-1332-1** · 🔺 **F-1331-4 (566 MB unstored, rule on `motion-pilot`)** · 🔺 **F-1331-2** · 🔺 **F-1330-1 (one word, rec (a))** · 🔺 **F-1329-1** · 🔺 **F-1328-3** · 🔺 **F-1327-1/F-1329-2** · 🔺 **F-1315-2** · 🔺 **F-1313-3** (rec (b)) · 🔺 **F-1313-2** · 🔺 **F-1312-2** · 🔺 `rf-34` (rec **A**) · 🔺 `vp-02e-jumper` · 🔺 `e1-hold-the-claim` · 🔺 **F-1302-2** · 🔺 F-1300-3 · 🔺 **F-1279-2 rec L2** · 🔺 F-1295-1 · 🔺 **F-1101-1 RETIRE THE THREAD** · 🔺 F-1120-2 (1b) · 🔺 F-1287-1 + F-1286-2 · 🔺 plist `ProcessType` (F-1270-4) · 🔺 F-1267-3 · 🔺 F-1242-1 + F-1193-2 · 🔺 F-1260-3 · 🔺 **F-1252-1** · 🔺 F-1257-4 · 🔺 F-1255-4/1 · 🔺 F-1254-3 · 🔺 F-1253-1/2/3 · 🔺 **F-1162-1 / F-1242-2 — 169th ask** · 🔺 F-1193-3 · 🔺 gold-per-token · 🔺 F-1209-3 · 🔺 TOWN ZOOM clamps · 🔺 F-1208-3 · 🔺 F-1204-1 · 🔺 **F-1141-3+F-1164-1** · 🔻 F-1182-2 **superseded by F-1335-2**. 💡 **THE THROUGH-LINE, AND IT IS s1334'S LESSON TURNED ONE TURN FURTHER: s1334 proved that a cure attached to a finding is a hypothesis wearing the finding's authority. The same is true of the FINDING itself once it is old enough.** A finding is a measurement with a timestamp; carrying it forward verbatim republishes a stale measurement under a fresh date, and marking it OWNER-GATED removes the only force that would ever re-check it. **F-1185-1 sat on the desk ~150 times behind a question whose answer had been in `lore/` for 18 days before it was asked.** Nothing was careless — every carry looked like diligence, and the desk list is *designed* to be carried verbatim. ➡️ **The asymmetry to remember: an owner-gated label is cheap to apply and enormously expensive to be wrong about, because it is the one label that stops anyone from looking again.** The nine-row backfill was supposed to be bookkeeping; measuring instead of transcribing turned it into a closed red, a corrected 151-times ask, and three guard defects. 🪞 **And the blade cut me too, twice: the case-sensitive grep that misled s1185 misled me identically, and my own ledger rows s