/Users/robin/Claude/Projects/Gold Rush/tasks/BACKLOG.md-19-⚠️ **F-2642-5 — I QUOTED A GUARD'S ERROR TEXT INSIDE THE LAW SURFACE AND ITS OWN PARSER READ MY QUOTATION AS A LIVE CITATION, TURNING A PASSING GUARD RED.** Re-basing a rotted pointer, I wrote the guard's drift line into my annotation verbatim — including the bare `<file>:<line>` token it prints. `law-pointer-guard` scans law surfaces for exactly that shape, so it picked the token out of my PROSE, tried to resolve it, and reported `UNRESOLVABLE … no such file` — because the guard prints a BARE basename while a resolvable citation needs its path. **A guard that had just PASSED was made to FAIL by the act of describing why it had failed.** ⚖️ Realised cost ZERO and NOT a false green — the failure is LOUD, and it was caught by the mandated closing battery (F-1300-4) rather than shipped. 🎯 **What earns it a row is WHERE it happens: a re-base annotation is the single most common law-surface edit a fire makes — this clause family alone records ~150 of them — and narrating the guard's own message is the most natural way to write one.** ➡️ **THEREFORE: when you annotate a pointer re-base, describe the guard's complaint in WORDS and never paste a `file:line` token you do not intend the parser to resolve.** I rewrote mine as *"its drift line named the FIRST member only"*, and the guard went to PASS with no other change. 💡 REUSABLE, and it is the known class (a guard's error text becomes a parser input) landing on a NEW SITE — not a tool reading another tool's output, but a LAW SURFACE quoting a tool that reads LAW SURFACES. **Any instrument whose corpus is prose will eventually read a fire's description of that instrument; the closer your prose sits to its subject, the more literally it is parsed.**
